nd  that's a gradual process of learning  call it acquisition i don't care what  you call it but that someone should take  the trouble to comment and say well no  you don't actually mean learning you  should be seeing acquisition  it's this whole  jargonization of this very natural  process of language learning that i find  very annoying it's as if we need some  special jargon for how to walk  you know or how to breathe we don't need  the jargon  they talk you know  s l a  second language x acquisition  uh there's e s l in which english has a  second language as opposed to e f l  english as a foreign language then  someone came up with aal english as  another language  who cares  the process of learning is essentially  the same  you can have a person in an esl  situation in other words someone say  living in canada trying to learn english  who has less contact with native  speakers than someone  living in  some foreign country where it's e f l  but where they have english speaking  friends whereas the immigrant here may  in fact only have chinese speaking  friends  so what does it matter e s l e f l a e a  l i don't know  uh there's all kinds of these terms like  inter language  you know  interlanguage supposedly is this idea  that we're between  the language our native language and the  language we're trying to learn so we  haven't totally  switched to this new language well what  does it matter  the the language as i learn another  language i am gradually progressing yes  my native language influences how i use  that language yes it will always  influence how i use that language in  terms of pronunciation in terms of my  choice of words and so forth but  gradually as i develop new patterns to  deal with the new language  i'm more and more comfortable in the new  language it's a constantly  it's a constant state of progression why  termite interlanguage no if i'm speaking  russian as poorly as i speak it i'm  speaking russian i'm not speaking  interlanguage it's the same with  fossilization it suggests that there's  some objective  um event or some some  thing that's outside the control of the  learner  that causes them to fossilize and not be  able to  progress further in the language i don't  believe that  i think that there are things that make  language learning difficult when you're  beginning and even at a later stage and  if you have the determination  and if you focus and if you're motivated  and if you like the language and if you  surround yourself  with  uh  native speakers you will continue to  improve in the language some people may  do better than others in pronunciation  in their use of words some have a bigger  vocabulary but they all have the ability  to continue to improve they are not  fossilized  unless they choose to or don't have the  interest or quite satisfied with their  existing level in the language but it's  a subjective thing it's not an objective  thing and i mean i can't remember all  these other terms there's lots of them  uh  so uh language acquisition language  learning i prefer the term learning  because it's a constant  random process  whereby the brain  gradually gets used to something i don't  believe the brain acquires the language  like now i have it it's mine i don't  believe that's the process at all  so let's not have too much jargon when  we talk about language learning it's  unavoidable that there is some  we see it in in  grammar where you know just to get back  to the subject when i learned chinese  and japanese  i mean there isn't much grammar in  chinese and i never remember any grammar  terms when i was learning chinese or  japanese but now they've  you know not to be outdone by the people  who do uh you know russian and all these  other languages they have to come up  with special terms to explain things  that are  don't need to be explained we don't need  the jargon i remember in in korean where  a lot of the verbs the endings imply  uh you know in order to or  because or there's a bunch of meaning  that comes with the ending so they come  at the end well i have a term for this  it's called copulative  verbs  like are you kidding me we don't need  that there's a verb it has this ending  it means this so i think i've gone on  long enough on this subject but i must  say when i saw that comment about  you really mean to say language  acquisition  it kind of  made me think about how much  the simple natural process of language  learning  is basically disrupted by all of these  supposedly well-meaning people  who invest so much time in coming up  with all kinds of complicated  explanations terminology and so forth i  am sure that will generate a lot of  criticism from my linguistics friends  thank you
 

